    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 19th with a projected lineup anchored by **Yordan Alvarez**, who provides our highest ceiling at a 65.56 efficiency score. While he faces a solid arm in Matthews, his elite xwOBA of .423 and favorable platoon alignment make him an automatic lock for the middle of the order. **Casey Schmitt** (59.53) and **Christian Yelich** (59.01) also project as strong starts; **Christian Yelich** is particularly intriguing today, as his matchup against Brown features a slow-delivery pitcher, providing a nuanced boost to his stolen base probability\u2014a critical factor given our strategy of chasing scarcity in the SB category. **Trea Turner** (56.61) remains a primary engine for the offense despite a difficult matchup against Burns, as his leadoff spot and inherent speed profile demand inclusion regardless of the opposing xERA.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today\u2019s docket involved balancing the bottom of the order against potential utility upside. **Caleb Durbin** (54.70) and **Hyeseong Kim** (55.86) secured the final spots over the remainder of the bench, largely due to Falter and Canning offering favorable SP skill metrics that outweigh the relative \"penalty\" of batting in the nine-hole. While **Junior Caminero** (50.78) holds a respectable score, his efficiency is suppressed by a high-difficulty matchup against Bradish, making him a marginal play compared to the platoon-advantaged **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (53.95). We are monitoring the status of **Luke Raley**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Josh Smith**, **Luis Pena**, and **Pedro Ramirez**, but none currently offer a higher projected daily value than our active core. With **Zac Veen** confirmed in the minors and **Kyle Schwarber**, **Colt Keith**, and **Gary Sanchez** unavailable, we are leaning into our current speed-heavy configuration to maximize our 5x5 category leverage against the day's pitching slate.",
